  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Systems · a long essay you can play with",
  "hero.title": "Drives Across a Wire",
  "hero.sub": "Your drive sits in another machine, so why can your computer treat it as its own?<br>Both are called network storage, so why is one called NAS and the other SAN, and why do data centres move NVMe onto the network?",
  "hero.lead": "Three questions are really one: <strong>once a drive leaves its enclosure and a network sits in between, what becomes expensive?</strong> The expensive part is the round trip. Every question waits for an answer, every lost packet has to be sent again, and every broken path needs another to take over. Starting from “what exactly are you asking the other side for?”, we go up layer by layer and see how each protocol spreads these costs: file-level NAS, block-level iSCSI and Fibre Channel, latency at every hop, TCP and RDMA, NVMe-oF, multipath, and finally cutting data into pieces spread across many machines. <strong>Each model is either a real rule from a specification, a measurement with its conditions stated, or a textbook result</strong>, scaled down to run in a web page.",
  "hero.note": "An AI wrote this page. Byte counts and defaults are taken directly from the specifications (RFC 7143, NVMe/TCP 1.1 and others); latencies come from published measurements that state their hardware and software versions. <strong>Where a value is marked “tuned parameter”, it was chosen so the model can be computed, not measured</strong>, and the text says how far the conclusion moves when that value is doubled or halved. This page covers mechanisms and specifications only, and <strong>does not rate any product as a purchase</strong>. Earlier essays in this series: <a href='../what-a-drive-is-made-of/'>“What a Drive Is Made Of”</a>, <a href='../how-sata-works/'>“Why SATA Has Lasted Over Twenty Years”</a>, <a href='../how-sas-works/'>“Why Server Drives Use SAS”</a> and <a href='../why-nvme-is-fast/'>“Why NVMe Is So Fast”</a>. Where this essay needs them, it only points back.",

  "toc.ch1": "Blocks, files, objects: what you ask for",
