  /* ── 01 ── */
  "ch1.title": "Blocks, files, objects: what you ask for",
  "ch1.law": "Network storage starts with one question: does the client ask for “block number N”, for “byte K of a file”, or for “a whole object”? The lower the level, the more the client manages itself, and the less can be shared.",
  "ch1.p1": "A home NAS and a SAN in a machine room both look like “a machine full of disks, connected by a network cable”. The difference is not the hardware but the question being asked. When you connect to a NAS, your computer says “open <code>photos/2025/IMG_0412.jpg</code> and read the first 64 KiB”. File names, directories and who may read are all managed by that machine, and what you see is a <strong>shared folder</strong>. When you connect to a SAN, your computer says “read block 123456”. The far side is just a numbered row of blocks, like a disk inside your own machine (see <a href='../what-a-drive-is-made-of/#ch1'>“What a Drive Is Made Of”, Chapter 1</a>). Building a file system on top, and keeping track of which file sits in which blocks, is entirely your computer’s job, and what you see is a <strong>new disk</strong>. Cloud object storage is a third kind of question: “give me the whole thing called <code>photos/0412</code>” or “replace the whole thing”. There is no directory tree, and you cannot change a few bytes in the middle.",
  "ch1.p2": "So block storage can be formatted with any file system and can run a database, but two computers cannot safely mount the same disk at once. Each file system assumes it is the only one making changes, and they overwrite each other’s writes (unless a dedicated cluster file system is used). File storage is shared by design, at the cost of asking the far side every time a file is opened. Object storage removes “change” altogether, leaving only whole-object writes and reads, which lets it span thousands of machines. The table below places common protocols on the map, in the same spirit as the layers in <a href='../what-a-drive-is-made-of/#ch7'>“What a Drive Is Made Of”, Chapter 7</a>: what the command says, what carries it, and which wire it runs on.",
